  "strategy": "## Approach and rationale\n\nThe core idea is to use a locally optimal solution obtained by Gurobi to generate a parametrized family of functions and then tune the parameters to find the best solution. This approach leverages Gurobi for integer programming to find a high-quality starting point, then refines it by optimizing continuous parameters.\n\nThe main rationale is that the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem is combinatorial in nature, and a mixed-integer programming formulation can provide a strong upper bound, which subsequently guides the search for continuous solutions. By parametrizing the solution and optimizing the parameters continuously, we can find a better solution than pure integer programming.\n\nLimitations:\n- Gurobi requires commercial license (though this might be available in academic settings)\n- The parametrization must be designed carefully to not miss the optimal structure\n- Combinatorial components (like avoiding certain overlaps) are approximated by continuous relaxation\n- Performance may depend on the specific parametrization chosen\n\nImportant considerations:\n- The formulation replaces periodic patterns by explicit constraints\n- Continuous parameters control the distribution rather explicitly specifying each bit\n- The transition from discrete solution to continuous relies on function space embeddings\n- We maintain validity of the optimization at all times\n\n## Mathematical specification\n\nWe represent h(x) as a step function with fixed height 1, defined by the positions of n $n$ transitions within [0,2]. Let $0 < x_1 < x_2 > ... > x_n < 2$ be the transition points. Then:\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\begin{cases} \n0 & x \\le x_1 \\\\\n1 & otherwise \n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nHowever, to allow continuous values we modify to:\n$$ h(x) = \\sigma(g(x) - t) $$\nwhere $\\sigma$ is the sigmoid function, $g(x)$ is a piecewise linear function, and $t$ is a threshold parameter.\n\nThe optimization formulation:\n\nVariables:\n- n transition points $x_1, x_2, ... , x_n \\in [0,2]$ with ordering constraint\n- Threshold $t$\n- Scale parameter $s$\n- Base parameter $b$\n\nConstraints:\n1. The function $h(x)$ must have an integral of exactly 1\n2. The function is increasing ($1$)\n3. The transitions $x_1,...,x_n$ are strictly increasing\n\nObjective:\nMinimize $\\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx$\n\n## Implementation procedure\n\nThe algorithm proceeds in two phases. Phase 1 (discrete) uses Gurobi to solve a mixed-integer program with explicit ordering and integral constraints. Phase 2 (continuous) takes this discrete solution and uses it as a guide for continuous parameter optimization.\n\n1. **Base Classes/Families**: Define several base families:\n   - Linear progression\n   - Piecewise constant\n   - Wave-like periodic approximators\n   - Randomized interleaving patterns\n\n2. **Discrete Phase**:\n   - Solve MIP formulation to determine transition positions\n   - Add combinatorial constraints to avoid bad overlaps\n   - Form a set of anchors that define the function shape\n   - Ensure strict ordering $0=x_1 < x_2 < ... < x_n=x_{n+1}=2$\n\n3. **Parametrization**:\n   - Represent solutions parametrically in terms of $k$ parameters\n   - These parameters control the positions, widths, and intensities\n   - Use translation and scaling transformations on base points\n\n4. **Continuous Optimization**:\n   - Implement a gradient-based optimizer with adaptive step sizes\n   - Use automatic differentiation through interpolation\n   - Add elastic constraints to maintain function validity\n   - Blend between base solutions at each optimization step\n\n5. **Fall backs**:\n   - Fallback to Thue-Morse construction if optimization fails\n   - Preserve the constraint $0\\leq h(x)\\leq1$ automatically\n   - Project solutions onto valid space with minimal distance penalty\n\n## Search choices and variations\n\nWe consider multiple search strategies:\n\n* Initial solution families:\n  - $\\text{Linear}(n)$: n evenly spaced points\n  - $\\text{Thue}(n)$: Fibonacci-like sequence\n  - $\\text{Random}(n)$: Random balanced sampling\n\n* Parameter \u03b7 (number of stages) can be:\n  - 1-10 (with exponential decay penalty for extremes)\n\n* Optimization algorithm:\n  - Difference method (depends on eta)\n  - BFGS adapatation\n  - Hessian-vector products for high-dimensional spaces\n\n* Potential modifications:\n  - Adjusting transition point distributions\n  - Multiple kernel families per optimization stage\n  - Progressive resolution (coarse to fine)\n\n## Numerical robustness\n\nEach candidate must:\n1. Satisfy $\\sum h(x)dx = 1$\n2. Maintain $0\\leq h(x)\\leq1$\n3. Have at most 128 parameters (computationally feasible)\n\nWe ensure numerical stability by:\n- Clipping values to $[0,1]$ after each iteration\n- Using $\\epsilon=1e-8$ for equality constraints\n- Implementing quadratic penalties for inequality violations\n\n## Runtimes and safeguard\n\nEach optimization iteration runs $\\eta$ stages maxing eta. Timeout detection through:\n\n- Careful counting of iterations \u00d7 dimension\n- Built-in gradient evaluation checks\n- Minimal projection of unvalidated solutions\n\nThe function is expected to run for up to 1100 seconds. If unresponsive, it falls back to:\n\n$$ h(x) = \\text{ThueMorse}(2m) \\text{ linear interpolated to } n \\text{ points} $$\n\nwith $n=\\min(512, 2\u00d7\\text{previous best})$.",
